[DOCUMENT]
TITLE: A view's verdict words, their colours and their meanings are rows in a legend narrative, never a grammar field and never renderer knowledge

[GRAMMAR]
IMPORT_FROM_FILE: @repo

[DECISION]
UID: DEC-LEGEND-IS-DATA
TITLE: A view's verdict words, their colours and their meanings are rows in a legend narrative, never a grammar field and never renderer knowledge
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATUS: accepted
RETIRES_ON: Two views need to share one verdict vocabulary and repeating the legend proves to drift.
STATEMENT: >>>
The words a view's rows may carry in their trailing bracket -- WORKS, GAP,
OPEN and the like -- are data. They live in one narrative whose WIDGET is
legend, a child of the root, one row per word in the form
"- WORD: colour: what the word means". The renderer reads the legend for
colours and labels; a colour is a name from the renderer's own small
vocabulary (green, slate, amber, blue, red, teal, violet, grey), each mapped
to a token that passes the colour-vision validator in both themes. A
bracket word that is not in the legend is a view-check finding.

There is no VERDICT field in the grammar. A verdict is a snapshot's opinion
about a line, not a node's state, and the vocabulary belongs to the view that
uses it: a whiteboard over the semantic layer says WORKS and FALLS OUT, the
rules index says STRICTDOC, SCRIPT and UNBUILT, and neither needs a grammar
edit to say so.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
A verdict field would have overlapped three rulings (the authorship ladder's
"seen and let stand", the open decision as the question, supersession as
liveness) and baked one project's words into the one grammar every project
shares. Colour by position was the cheaper alternative and was rejected
because the operator asked for the way data is shown to be feedable from
sdoc.
<<<
NOTES: >>>
Ruled 2026-08-30 as a first-pass decision under the operator's delegation of that day ("I'll trust you to take first pass at making decisions"); overrule by superseding. AUTHORED_BY reads llm because the writer has no flag for the human rungs.
<<<
